1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,487
Here is the uncomfortable arithmetic of quantum computing.

2
00:00:03,487 --> 00:00:05,400
A physical qubit is bad.

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:06,412
It fails.

4
00:00:06,412 --> 00:00:07,512
Not occasionally.

5
00:00:07,512 --> 00:00:08,462
Constantly.

6
00:00:08,462 --> 00:00:09,850
Coherence decays.

7
00:00:09,850 --> 00:00:11,125
Gates are imperfect.

8
00:00:11,125 --> 00:00:12,562
Readout is noisy.

9
00:00:12,562 --> 00:00:18,687
The best qubits in the world, in twenty twenty-four, had error rates around one in a thousand per gate.

10
00:00:18,687 --> 00:00:19,725
That sounds good.

11
00:00:19,725 --> 00:00:21,112
It is catastrophic.

12
00:00:21,112 --> 00:00:24,050
A useful algorithm might need a billion gates.

13
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,975
At one in a thousand, the computation is destroyed long before it finishes.

14
00:00:28,975 --> 00:00:31,625
Even at one in ten thousand, you lose.

15
00:00:31,625 --> 00:00:35,250
The solution is not better qubits, though better qubits help.

16
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:46,387
The solution is to stop using physical qubits and start using logical ones: a logical qubit made of many physical qubits, wired together so that they correct each other's errors.

17
00:00:46,387 --> 00:00:51,312
This is quantum error correction, and it is the most important idea in the field.

18
00:00:51,312 --> 00:00:53,787
The simplest version is the surface code.

19
00:00:53,787 --> 00:00:55,762
Imagine a grid of qubits.

20
00:00:55,762 --> 00:00:56,837
Some hold data.

21
00:00:56,837 --> 00:01:00,612
Some are measured constantly, checking the parity of their neighbours.

22
00:01:00,612 --> 00:01:03,987
When a measurement flips, you know something went wrong nearby.

23
00:01:03,987 --> 00:01:06,262
You don't know what, and you don't need to.

24
00:01:06,262 --> 00:01:09,912
You just need enough checks to localise the error and correct it.

25
00:01:09,912 --> 00:01:13,975
The catch: you need a lot of physical qubits per logical qubit.

26
00:01:13,975 --> 00:01:15,900
Early estimates were thousands.

27
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:17,600
Later ones, hundreds.

28
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:22,262
And the whole scheme only works if the physical error rate is below a threshold.

29
00:01:22,262 --> 00:01:24,850
Roughly one percent, for the surface code.

30
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:28,462
Above the threshold, adding more qubits makes things worse.

31
00:01:28,462 --> 00:01:31,337
Below it, adding more qubits makes things better.

32
00:01:31,337 --> 00:01:32,612
That is the whole game.

33
00:01:32,612 --> 00:01:34,800
Get below threshold, and scale.

34
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:38,037
For twenty years, nobody could get below threshold.

35
00:01:38,037 --> 00:01:43,025
The qubits were too noisy, the grids too small, the measurements too slow.

36
00:01:43,025 --> 00:01:47,362
In December twenty twenty-four, Google published a result that changed that.

37
00:01:47,362 --> 00:01:48,962
The machine was called Willow.

38
00:01:48,962 --> 00:01:58,937
It had one hundred and five superconducting qubits, and it was designed to test one thing: whether increasing the size of the error-correcting grid actually reduced the error.

39
00:01:58,937 --> 00:02:02,800
They built grids of distance three, five, and seven.

40
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:09,350
A distance seven surface code spends ninety-seven physical qubits to carry one logical qubit.

41
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:11,225
Forty-nine of them hold the data.

42
00:02:11,225 --> 00:02:13,062
The rest do nothing but check.

43
00:02:13,062 --> 00:02:18,387
And they measured the logical error rate per cycle: zero point one four three percent.

44
00:02:18,387 --> 00:02:20,837
That number by itself is not impressive.

45
00:02:20,837 --> 00:02:22,450
What matters is the trend.

46
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:29,250
Each time they increased the distance by two, the logical error rate fell by a factor of about two point one four.

47
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:30,437
Not stayed the same.

48
00:02:30,437 --> 00:02:31,137
Fell.

49
00:02:31,137 --> 00:02:36,200
For the first time, a quantum error-correcting code was performing better as it got bigger.

50
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:37,537
Below threshold.

51
00:02:37,537 --> 00:02:41,037
The thing the theory promised, in hardware, on a chip.

52
00:02:41,037 --> 00:02:45,262
The same paper included a second result, and this one made headlines.

53
00:02:45,262 --> 00:02:49,675
Willow ran a random circuit sampling benchmark in under five minutes.

54
00:02:49,675 --> 00:02:55,937
Google estimated that the same calculation would take a classical supercomputer ten septillion years.

55
00:02:55,937 --> 00:02:58,487
A one followed by twenty-five zeros.

56
00:02:58,487 --> 00:03:00,662
That number is almost meaningless.

57
00:03:00,662 --> 00:03:06,325
It is a statement about one benchmark, on one day, against one classical algorithm.

58
00:03:06,325 --> 00:03:08,825
It is not a statement about useful computing.

59
00:03:08,825 --> 00:03:11,050
Google was careful about this in the paper.

60
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:12,275
The press was not.

61
00:03:12,275 --> 00:03:15,325
But the error-correction result is not meaningless.

62
00:03:15,325 --> 00:03:18,525
It is the first real evidence that the scaling law works.

63
00:03:18,525 --> 00:03:23,687
And scaling laws are how technology stops being a demonstration and starts being a tool.

64
00:03:23,687 --> 00:03:26,562
There is a catch, and it is worth saying clearly.

65
00:03:26,562 --> 00:03:31,637
A logical qubit that fails once every thousand cycles is not a good qubit.

66
00:03:31,637 --> 00:03:36,500
Willow's logical qubit still fails far too often to run a long algorithm.

67
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:40,312
The result is a proof of direction, not a proof of arrival.

68
00:03:40,312 --> 00:03:52,812
To run Shor's algorithm against a two thousand bit RSA key, you would need thousands of logical qubits, each made of hundreds of physical qubits, each operating for millions of cycles without failing.

69
00:03:52,812 --> 00:03:55,412
That is not one generation of hardware away.

70
00:03:55,412 --> 00:03:56,575
It might be three.

71
00:03:56,575 --> 00:03:59,412
And yet, the direction is now established.

72
00:03:59,412 --> 00:04:02,187
Before Willow, below-threshold was a hope.

